<exercise>
<p>
  Use the algorithm "Double the tree" for the following Traveling Salesman Problem. You need to show all the steps of the algorithm: 
  <ol>
    <li>
        <p>
        Find a minimum spanning tree (any algorithm you want);     
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
          Double the tree;  
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
         Find an Eulerian circuit for the doubled tree   
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
          Follow the Eulerian circuit, taking shortcuts 
  as necessary to avoid repeating vertices.  (Note that the graph does not satisfy the triangle inequality; ignore this.)
        </p>
    </li>
  </ol>  
  The output should be a Hamiltonian cycle. Give the cycle by enumerating the vertices, and also compute the total weight. 
</p>
  <image source="TSP.jpg" width="50%">
    <shortdescription>a graph</shortdescription>
  </image>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <p>
    Show that the square <m>P_n^2</m> of the path graph <m>P_n</m> is Hamiltonian for all <m>n\ge 3</m>. 
    </p>
<hint>
  <p>
    You need to consider two cases, depending on whether <m>n</m> is odd or even. To build intuition, you may consider the cases <m>n=4</m> and <m>n=5</m> first.   
 However, try to write a proof that works for all <m>n\ge 3</m>. 
  </p>
  </hint>
</exercise>

</exercises> 

  
